  "narrative": "Due response-deadline and new validation evidence assessed.\n\nH125 (PTEN loss in mutation-burden-low cells; R96/R105) is accepted and closed. The claim direction is negative, so the positive signed estimates represent a lower/more-negative dependency_composite score in PTEN-loss cells. Discovery gave +0.283 [0.155, 0.410], entirely above the 0.15-unit threshold. Independent validation gave +0.264 [0.081, 0.447]; the validation lower bound is below the effect-size threshold but excludes zero under the strict validation alpha, and the point estimate is nearly identical to discovery. The combination of a decisive discovery interval, consistent replicated magnitude, and the broader accepted PTEN-loss pattern supports a moderate supplied-dataset association in low mutation-burden contexts. This is not causal and does not establish generalizability.\n\nH74 (PTEN loss within the KY CRISPR library; R43/R106) remains rejected and closed. Discovery gave +0.179 [-0.003, 0.361] and validation gave +0.187 [-0.085, 0.459]. Both intervals cross zero and the meaningful-effect threshold, so although the point estimates are in the claimed direction, neither analysis establishes a robust PTEN-loss effect in the KY-library context. This does not contradict the broader PTEN-loss association; it specifically rejects the KY-context boundary as unsupported.\n\nH151 (PTEN loss in high aneuploidy; R114/R132) is set to unresolved and closed as the original boundary claim. Discovery gave +0.271 [0.087, 0.455], suggesting a lower/more-negative score but with the lower bound below the 0.15-unit threshold. Validation gave +0.208 [-0.029, 0.446], consistent in magnitude but crossing zero under the strict validation interval. The evidence is directionally encouraging but not sufficiently precise to accept a high-aneuploidy-specific PTEN effect, and it should not be included among the supported PTEN boundary findings.\n\nOverall supported supplied-dataset findings remain: TP53 loss is robustly associated with a higher/less-negative composite score around +0.80 [R3], KEAP1 loss around +0.81 [R6], SMARCA4 loss with a lower/more-negative score around +0.80 in claimed-negative direction [R33], MSI-high with a lower/more-negative score around +0.77 [R51], and PTEN loss with a moderate lower/more-negative association generally around +0.24 to +0.28 when supported by cleaner or validated low-burden contexts [R35/R38, R40, R86/R92, R96/R105]. These are associations in the supplied data, not causal or external-validity claims.\n\nMajor negative or unsupported findings include lineage/sex main effects, most constructed marker/signature interactions, additional measured driver comparisons in TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4/PTEN wild-type cells [R120-R126], the squamous SMARCA4 interaction after validation failure [R110/R119], PTEN loss in the KY library [R43/R106], PTEN loss interacting with high aneuploidy [R128], TP53-by-EMT [R129], KEAP1-by-hypoxia [R130], and MSI-high-by-Wnt [R131]. These results indicate broad context stability for the dominant signals and no strong support for the tested modifiers.\n\nThe main unresolved issue is PTEN context specificity, especially high aneuploidy. The most informative next analysis is the newly registered refinement of H151: estimate PTEN loss specifically in high-aneuploidy cells that also lack TP53, KEAP1, SMARCA4, and MSI-high status. This should reduce dominant-driver and MSI confounding and better distinguish whether PTEN has an independent high-aneuploidy boundary effect or whether the broad PTEN association is the only reliable pattern.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 10 synthesis final assessment. H125 accepted/closed: validated moderate PTEN-loss lower/more-negative dependency in mutation-burden-low cells, point ~0.26-0.28; validation excludes zero but not threshold, accepted on strong discovery plus replication. H74 rejected/closed: KY-library PTEN-loss effect unsupported, intervals cross zero/threshold. H151 unresolved/closed: high-aneuploidy PTEN-loss estimates positive but imprecise; validation crosses zero; do not accept boundary-specific PTEN effect. Supported dominant pattern unchanged: TP53 loss ~+0.8 higher/less-negative, KEAP1 loss ~+0.8 higher/less-negative, SMARCA4 loss ~-0.8 lower/more-negative, MSI-high ~-0.77 lower/more-negative, PTEN loss moderate ~-0.24 to -0.28 lower/more-negative in clean/validated low-burden contexts. Negative/unsupported: lineage/sex, most markers/signatures, additional drivers in quadruple-WT cells, squamous SMARCA4 interaction, PTEN KY context, PTEN high-aneuploidy interaction, EMT/hypoxia/Wnt and many other context interactions. Unresolved: PTEN high-aneuploidy boundary. Proposed refinement: PTEN loss in high aneuploidy among TP53-, KEAP1-, SMARCA4-, MSI-low cells to test independent context-specific PTEN association. All conclusions are supplied-dataset associations, not causal or generalizable claims."
